<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="act-temp">
  <title>Continuous Functions on Closed Intervals</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Let <m>f: [a,b] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> be a continuous function, and let <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m>. Recall that for each <m>c \in [a,b]</m>, we say that <m>\delta &gt; 0</m> is <em>acceptable</em> if whenever <m>|x-c| &lt; \delta</m> we have <m>|f(x)-f(c)| &lt; \epsilon</m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that there exists some <m>\delta &gt; 0</m> that is acceptable for all <m>c \in [a,b]</m>. Prove that <m>f</m> is uniformly continuous.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that there is no <m>\delta &gt; 0</m> that is acceptable for all <m>c \in [a,b]</m>.
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    For each <m>n \in \mathbb{N}</m>, we know that <m>\frac{1}{n}</m> cannot be acceptable for all <m>c \in [a,b]</m> (although it can be acceptable for some of them). State precisely what this means.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    For each <m>n \in \mathbb{N}</m>, let <m>c_{n}</m> be the <q><m>c</m></q> you found in (a), and let <m>x_{n}</m> be the <q><m>x</m></q> you found in (a). Prove that the sequence <m>\{ c_{n} \}</m> is bounded.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Prove that <m>\{ c_{n} \}</m> has a convergent subsequence <m>\{ c_{p_n}\}</m>, and prove that <m>\{ x_{p_n}\}</m> converges to the same number as <m>\{ c_{p_n}\}</m>. Hint: <m>x_{p_n}= (x_{p_n}- c_{p_n}) + c_{p_n}</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Find the limit of <m>|f(x_{p_n}) - f(c_{p_n}|</m>. Can you find the contradiction?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>